  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{2}}\\,?\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2-2=0.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)-(tx)^{2}}{3(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}xy-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{3{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(xy-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{0}=1,$ the equality $g(tx,ty)=g(x,y)$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{0}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0.$",
